1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,856
Suppose the platform team of a bank sets up heeyID for the first time, in a quiet data centre.

2
00:00:05,906 --> 00:00:10,728
One line is missing from the configuration: the lifetime of a one-time code.

3
00:00:10,778 --> 00:00:16,088
What should the platform do: guess a number, or stop?

4
00:00:16,138 --> 00:00:22,544
Lesson 3 gave each bank its own installation, inside its own perimeter, in two zones.

5
00:00:22,594 --> 00:00:29,048
Each installation needs its own values, and this lesson shows where they come from and how they change.

6
00:00:29,098 --> 00:00:30,920
Many systems would guess.

7
00:00:30,970 --> 00:00:35,624
Somewhere in the code there is a constant, and the constant wins, quietly.

8
00:00:35,674 --> 00:00:38,840
In heeyID, the code has no default value at all.

9
00:00:38,890 --> 00:00:42,560
A default hidden in the code is a decision that nobody signed.

10
00:00:42,610 --> 00:00:47,384
Nobody sees it, nobody approves it, and nobody can say when it changed.

11
00:00:47,434 --> 00:00:53,000
So the values live in one place: the OpenTofu configuration of the installation.

12
00:00:53,050 --> 00:00:57,032
OpenTofu is the tool that describes the installation as code.

13
00:00:57,082 --> 00:01:05,408
Its catalogue holds one value for each policy, and the default texts for consent, terms and notifications, in four languages.

14
00:01:05,458 --> 00:01:10,352
At setup, the platform reads this catalogue and writes it as version zero.

15
00:01:10,402 --> 00:01:17,000
The proposer of version zero is the product, and each row has a mark that says: this is still the default.

16
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:22,208
If one value is missing, the setup stops, with a clear error that names the value.

17
00:01:22,258 --> 00:01:29,552
So the platform team sees the missing code lifetime in the first minute, not in a complaint from a customer a month later.

18
00:01:29,602 --> 00:01:32,192
There is a second gate before the first customer.

19
00:01:32,242 --> 00:01:38,168
The installation does not start its service until the bank has at least one active app with a required level.

20
00:01:38,218 --> 00:01:42,992
Until then, every journey answers: the service is not available.

21
00:01:43,042 --> 00:01:45,944
Two kinds of values sit in that configuration.

22
00:01:45,994 --> 00:01:53,072
Policies are the rules of the bank for each customer journey: code lifetimes, attempt limits and waiting times.

23
00:01:53,122 --> 00:02:03,920
Installation settings belong to one zone: the hosts, the zone itself, whether test customers are allowed, the call timeouts, the breaker values and the access token lifetime.

24
00:02:03,970 --> 00:02:07,664
A policy has versions, a history and the default mark.

25
00:02:07,714 --> 00:02:12,944
An installation setting is how one zone runs, and a change needs a new deployment.

26
00:02:12,994 --> 00:02:17,048
Later, the bank wants a shorter lifetime for one-time codes.

27
00:02:17,098 --> 00:02:22,784
Nobody changes it in the administrator panel, because the panel changes no policy and no setting.

28
00:02:22,834 --> 00:02:28,304
The platform team changes one line in the OpenTofu configuration, and applies it.

29
00:02:28,354 --> 00:02:31,424
The platform checks the new value before it uses it.

30
00:02:31,474 --> 00:02:38,024
It refuses zero, a negative number, a wrong unit, or a value outside its allowed range.

31
00:02:38,074 --> 00:02:40,184
A typo cannot become a policy.

32
00:02:40,234 --> 00:02:45,848
A valid value becomes a new version of the policy, and the earlier version stays in its history.

33
00:02:45,898 --> 00:02:51,416
Texts follow the same path: a new consent text comes from the configuration, as a new version.

34
00:02:51,466 --> 00:02:53,288
Back to the missing line.

35
00:02:53,338 --> 00:02:58,040
The setup stopped, the team added the value, and version zero was complete.

36
00:02:58,090 --> 00:03:02,552
The shorter code lifetime became version one, with its time and its source.

37
00:03:02,602 --> 00:03:05,888
Every value has a source, a version and an owner.

38
00:03:05,938 --> 00:03:11,024
That is why this course says the configured time or the configured limit, and never a number.

39
00:03:11,074 --> 00:03:12,896
The number belongs to the bank.

40
00:03:12,946 --> 00:03:21,064
Check yourself: the bank wants heeyID to wait longer for TAF, so where does that change go?

41
00:03:21,114 --> 00:03:28,864
It is a call timeout, so it is an installation setting: the platform team changes it in the configuration of that zone, with a new deployment.

42
00:03:28,914 --> 00:03:30,736
There is no default in the code.

43
00:03:30,786 --> 00:03:35,848
The defaults and the texts come from the OpenTofu configuration, as version zero.

44
00:03:35,898 --> 00:03:38,728
A missing or a wrong value stops the setup.

45
00:03:38,778 --> 00:03:44,632
Policies and installation settings change only in the configuration, never in the panel.

46
00:03:44,682 --> 00:03:52,840
Some installation settings are timeouts: how long heeyID waits for MyID, TAF or the SMS vendor.

47
00:03:52,890 --> 00:03:57,568
Lesson 5 shows what heeyID does when one of them is slow, or down.
